{"ex-203-1":{"number":"1","title":"Drawing free-body diagrams","question":"<p>Draw a free-body diagram for each of the following objects.</p><p>(a) A ball falling freely (b) A box being pushed to the left</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/a0e4594f3a30606ea20bcef7.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:430.9px\"/></p>","answer":"<p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/7e1dca67bccc387cf98365bb.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:453.9px\"/></p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 203  |  Source PDF p. 6","source":{"name":"203 Examples.docx","sha256":"e9e235b049c1e37e6ec96b65697515faf34540532a5437bbb222c4c9904a492c","block":2},"id":"ex-203-1","chapter":"203"},"ex-203-2":{"number":"2","title":"Man inside a lift","question":"<p>A man is standing in a lift which is moving downwards at a constant velocity.</p><p>(a) Name the forces acting on the man.</p><p>(b) Is there any net force acting on the man? Explain your answer.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/5caeb30b0000caf19241ee88.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:188.6px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>(a) Weight and normal force acting by the floor of the lift</p><p>(b) Since he is moving at a constant velocity with the lift, by Newton’s first law of motion, there is no net force acting on him.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 203  |  Source PDF p. 14","source":{"name":"203 Examples.docx","sha256":"e9e235b049c1e37e6ec96b65697515faf34540532a5437bbb222c4c9904a492c","block":9},"id":"ex-203-2","chapter":"203"},"ex-203-3":{"number":"3","title":"Acceleration of a puck","question":"<p>Tom hits an ice hockey puck to the right with a horizontal force of 100 N. The mass of the puck is 0.17 kg. The friction acting on the puck is negligible. Find the acceleration of the puck.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/81ef7e0abdd1ed2a1e905118.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:250.4px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>Flow chart for this example:</p><p>draw free-body diagram</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/8108df797de742694ad5743d.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:113.5px\"/></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = 100 N (rightwards)</p><p>By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 588 m s<sup>−2</sup> (rightwards)</p><p>relate all forces acting on an object to the net force to find F<sub>net</sub></p><p>apply F<sub>net</sub> = ma to find a</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>F<sub>net</sub> acts along the moving direction. We need to consider only the force(s) acting along this direction when applying F<sub>net</sub> = ma. The weight and the normal force cancel each other out.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 203  |  Source PDF p. 21","source":{"name":"203 Examples.docx","sha256":"e9e235b049c1e37e6ec96b65697515faf34540532a5437bbb222c4c9904a492c","block":18},"id":"ex-203-3","chapter":"203"},"ex-203-4":{"number":"4","title":"Water skiing","question":"<p>Susan of mass 55 kg is pulled by a horizontal rope and accelerates towards the left along a straight path. The tension in the rope is 210 N. The resistive force acting on her by water and air is 100 N. Her velocity at time</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0 is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(a) What is Susan’s acceleration?</p><p>(b) Susan travels 6 m from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0 to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 1 s.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/376603bfd4997eb4d3283953.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:208.3px\"/></p><p>Find <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(c) Susan lets go of the rope suddenly. Find her acceleration right after letting go.</p>","answer":"<p>Flow chart for parts (a) and (b):</p><p>draw free-body diagram</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/5edd211153d0e0c0d66dc266.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:289.7px\"/></p><p>relate all forces acting on an object to the net force to find F<sub>net</sub> Take leftwards as positive.</p><p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> + (−<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> ) = 210 + (−100) = 110 N</p><p>By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>,</p><p>apply F<sub>net</sub> = ma to find a</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 2 m s<sup>−2</sup></p><p>Susan’s acceleration is 2 m s<sup>−2</sup> (leftwards).</p><p>(b) By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></math>,</p><p>apply equation of motion to find u</p><p>6 = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> × 1 + <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 2 × 1<sup>2</sup></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 5 m s<sup>−1</sup></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>u</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is 5 m s<sup>−1</sup> (leftwards).</p><p>(c) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = −<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = −100 N</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = −1.82 m s<sup>−2</sup></p><p>Susan’s acceleration is 1.82 m s<sup>−2</sup> (rightwards).</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>F<sub>net</sub> acts along Susan’s moving direction. We need to consider only the force(s) acting along this direction when applying F<sub>net</sub> = ma. Note that T and f represent the magnitudes of forces. You need to add a negative sign if a force points to the negative direction.</p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p>After Susan lets go of the rope, the tension no longer acts on Susan and the resistive force becomes the net force acting on Susan.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 203  |  Source PDF p. 22","source":{"name":"203 Examples.docx","sha256":"e9e235b049c1e37e6ec96b65697515faf34540532a5437bbb222c4c9904a492c","block":36},"id":"ex-203-4","chapter":"203"},"ex-203-5":{"number":"5","title":"Weight of an astronaut on the Moon","question":"<p>An astronaut has a mass of 80 kg. When he is on the Moon, his weight is one-sixth of that on the Earth.</p><p>(a) Find his weight on the Earth and on the Moon.</p><p>(b) Find the acceleration due to gravity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>M</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> on the Moon.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) Weight on the Earth <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>W</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>E</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 80 × 9.81 = 785 N</p><p>Weight on the Moon <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>W</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>M</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>W</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>E</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × 785 = 131 N</p><p>(b) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>M</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>W</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>M</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 1.64 m s<sup>−2</sup></p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 203  |  Source PDF p. 27","source":{"name":"203 Examples.docx","sha256":"e9e235b049c1e37e6ec96b65697515faf34540532a5437bbb222c4c9904a492c","block":70},"id":"ex-203-5","chapter":"203"},"ex-203-6":{"number":"6","title":"Worker on a platform","question":"<p>A 60-kg worker is standing on a platform (Fig a). Figure b shows the free- body diagram for the worker.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/7598ba3bfc63b294c64c77d7.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:469.7px\"/></p><p>(a) Find the magnitude of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>W</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and name force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(b) Find the magnitude of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> when the platform is moving upwards</p><p>(i) with a constant velocity,</p><p>(ii) with an acceleration of 0.2 m s<sup>−2</sup>.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>W</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 60 × 9.81 = 589 N</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is the normal force exerted by the platform.</p><p>(b) (i) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>W</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 589 N</p><p>(ii) Take upwards as positive. By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> + (−<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>W</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>) = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> − 589 = 60 × 1.2</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 661 N</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>No net force during uniform motion</p><p>Editorial check</p><p>Editorial check: The printed solution is retained above. Part (b)(ii) states an acceleration of 0.2 m s<sup>-2</sup>, whereas the printed solution substitutes 1.2 m s<sup>-2</sup>. Using the stated acceleration gives A = 60(9.81 + 0.2) = 601 N.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 203  |  Source PDF p. 27","source":{"name":"203 Examples.docx","sha256":"e9e235b049c1e37e6ec96b65697515faf34540532a5437bbb222c4c9904a492c","block":79},"id":"ex-203-6","chapter":"203"},"ex-203-7":{"number":"7","title":"Feeling of weight in a lift","question":"<p>In a stationary lift at a high tower, a girl steps on a weighing scale calibrated in newtons. The scale reads</p><p>500 N. The lift then starts from rest at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0 and takes</p><p>37 s to travel from the 5th floor to the 89th floor. The reading of the scale is as shown. Find the acceleration of the lift from (a) 0−15 s, and (b) 22−37 s.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/8ad52790b8d3457d22f3be39.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:337.1px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>Take upwards as positive.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>W</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>g</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 50.97 kg</p><p>(a) From 0−15 s,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> + (−<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>W</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> ) = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p>557 + (−500) = 50.97<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/ad78af011f6ab337e9bb2f15.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:87.0px\"/></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 1.12 m s<sup>−2</sup></p><p>The acceleration of the lift from 0–15 s is 1.12 m s<sup>−2</sup> (upwards).</p><p>(b) From 22−37 s,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> + (−<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>W</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>) = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p>443 + (−500) = 50.97<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = −1.12 m s<sup>−2</sup></p><p>The acceleration of the lift from 22–37 s is 1.12 m s<sup>−2</sup> (downwards).</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 203  |  Source PDF p. 31","source":{"name":"203 Examples.docx","sha256":"e9e235b049c1e37e6ec96b65697515faf34540532a5437bbb222c4c9904a492c","block":100},"id":"ex-203-7","chapter":"203"},"ex-203-8":{"number":"8","title":"Landing of Perseverance","question":"<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>r</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>c</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is a robotic rover sent to Mars. The spacecraft holding Perseverance was slowed down by fluid resistance in the atmosphere of Mars. When it was 11 km above the surface of Mars, a parachute was opened.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/f98f2edcae901ea929ffcb54.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:454.3px\"/></p><p>(a) Suppose the spacecraft was moving vertically downwards with a deceleration of 50 m s<sup>–2</sup> at a certain instant. Take the mass and weight of the spacecraft as 3890 kg and 14 400 N respectively. Find the fluid resistance acting on the spacecraft.</p><p>(b) How does the fluid resistance acting on the spacecraft change with time after the parachute is opened?</p>","answer":"<p>(a) Take upwards as positive.</p><p>By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> + (−<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>W</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>) = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> + (−14 400) = 3890 × 50</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 209 000 N</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/81e46f4c4ee11d8475065ea9.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:103.4px\"/></p><p>(b) The fluid resistance decreases until reaching a value equal to the weight of the spacecraft. Then it becomes constant.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 203  |  Source PDF p. 35","source":{"name":"203 Examples.docx","sha256":"e9e235b049c1e37e6ec96b65697515faf34540532a5437bbb222c4c9904a492c","block":120},"id":"ex-203-8","chapter":"203"},"ex-203-9":{"number":"9","title":"Friction and applied force","question":"<p>A 2-kg box resting on a rough horizontal plane is pulled towards the right by a horizontal force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. The friction acting on it is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/9078eb02ea38e04590eff64c.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:303.0px\"/></p><p>The box remains stationary when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is 1 N and accelerates at 3 m s<sup>−2</sup> to the</p><p>right when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 10 N.</p><p>(a) Find the magnitude of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is 1 N.</p><p>(b) Find the magnitude of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is 10 N.</p><p>(c) Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is reduced continuously from 10 N to 0 in a short period of time. The box keeps moving to the right within this period.</p><p>(i) Find the acceleration <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> of the box when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0.</p><p>(ii) Sketch a graph of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> against <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> within the above period.</p>","answer":"<p>Take rightwards as positive.</p><p>(a) Since the box is stationary, by Newton’s first law of motion,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 1 N</p><p>(b) By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> + (−<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> ) = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p>10 − <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 2 × 3</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 4 N</p><p>(c) (i) When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0, the box keeps moving to the right.</p><p>∴ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 4 N and points leftwards.</p><p>By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>e</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>F</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> + (−<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> ) = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>m</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p>0 + (−4) = 2<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = −2 m s<sup>−2</sup></p><p>The acceleration is 2 m s<sup>−2</sup> (leftwards).</p><p>(ii)</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/af338c8fb3ddfcddea808016.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:193.3px\"/></p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>F + (−f) = ma</p><p>F + (−4) = 2a</p><p>a = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>F − 2</p><p>a varies linearly with F.</p><p>When F = 0, a = −2 m s<sup>−2</sup></p><p>When F = 10 N, a = 3 m s<sup>−2</sup></p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 203  |  Source PDF p. 40","source":{"name":"203 Examples.docx","sha256":"e9e235b049c1e37e6ec96b65697515faf34540532a5437bbb222c4c9904a492c","block":134},"id":"ex-203-9","chapter":"203"},"ex-203-10":{"number":"10","title":"Launch of a rocket","question":"<p>Explain how a rocket is launched by ejecting hot gas downwards.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/4f07ace153757e6feaeb8e75.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:307.0px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>The rocket exerts a force on the gas to push the gas downwards. By Newton’s third law, the gas exerts an equal but opposite reaction force to push the rocket upwards.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 203  |  Source PDF p. 43","source":{"name":"203 Examples.docx","sha256":"e9e235b049c1e37e6ec96b65697515faf34540532a5437bbb222c4c9904a492c","block":170},"id":"ex-203-10","chapter":"203"},"ex-203-11":{"number":"11","title":"Pushing a car","question":"<p>A man is pushing a car but the car does not move.</p><p>(a) If the pushing force on the car by the man is the action force, what is the reaction force?</p><p>(b) Explain why the car is stationary.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/331f7319934145257cf24c0f.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:220.0px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>(a) The force acting on the man by the car</p><p>(b) The friction acting on the car by the ground and the pushing force by the man cancel each other out and therefore the net force acting on the car is zero. By Newton’s first law, the car remains stationary.</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>Consider the forces acting on the car when analysing the motion of the car.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/c99ba89c4b1e963900063233.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:188.6px\"/></p>","think":"<p>Samuel says that the car is stationary because the pushing force on the car by the man is cancelled out by the reaction force in (a). Do you agree?</p>","thinkAnswer":"<p>No. The pushing force acts on the car, whereas its reaction acts on the man. Forces on different objects cannot cancel in the force balance for the car. The car remains stationary because the ground exerts static friction on the car that balances the pushing force.</p>","location":"Book 2  |  Chapter 203  |  Source PDF p. 44","source":{"name":"203 Examples.docx","sha256":"e9e235b049c1e37e6ec96b65697515faf34540532a5437bbb222c4c9904a492c","block":176},"id":"ex-203-11","chapter":"203"}}